WEBVTT

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:06.283
♪ Come together now ♪

00:00:06.283 --> 00:00:08.951
[CAR HORNS HONKING]

00:00:08.951 --> 00:00:11.413
ALL: Whoa!

00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:13.039
Never gets old.

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:16.376
If we pull this off, we'll be on posters all over town.

00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:19.588
Why are you up there, Mrs. Tiggy-Winkle?

00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:22.090
Eye candy.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.259
[SNORING]

00:00:25.677 --> 00:00:27.345
Cock-a-doodle... Ahem.

00:00:27.345 --> 00:00:29.431
Cock-a-doodle...

00:00:29.431 --> 00:00:30.641
[HOARSELY] Oh, no.

00:00:30.641 --> 00:00:32.350
I've lost my voice.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.060
Wake up. Wake up.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.440
We've got to make the giant ball of fire rise into the...

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.150
What?

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.735
How is this possible?

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:43.487
It was all a hoax

00:00:43.487 --> 00:00:47.282
to keep me from questioning my very existence.

00:00:47.282 --> 00:00:50.744
My life's been one big lie!

00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:54.623
Wait. But maybe this means I can fly.

00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:56.291
Maybe I can fly!

00:00:56.291 --> 00:00:58.168
[YELLING]

00:00:58.168 --> 00:00:59.127
[THUDS]

00:00:59.127 --> 00:01:00.587
[STRAINING] No.

00:01:00.587 --> 00:01:04.675
Cock-a-doodle-doo! Cock-a-doodle-doo!

00:01:04.675 --> 00:01:07.845
NARRATOR: As the new day dawned on the empty farm,

00:01:07.845 --> 00:01:09.596
it wasn't just Peter and the others

00:01:09.596 --> 00:01:11.682
off on a journey of self-discovery.

00:01:11.682 --> 00:01:15.059
Bea was also answering the knock of opportunity.

00:01:15.059 --> 00:01:16.478
"Peter, Flopsy, Mopsy,

00:01:16.478 --> 00:01:18.313
Cotton-Tail and Cousin Benjamin,

00:01:18.313 --> 00:01:20.691
with help from the others, rebuilt the cottage,

00:01:20.691 --> 00:01:23.193
and they all lived happily ever after."

00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:25.153
[CROWD APPLAUDING]

00:01:25.153 --> 00:01:26.488
[CHUCKLES]

00:01:26.488 --> 00:01:28.532
[CROWD CHATTERING]

00:01:28.532 --> 00:01:30.575
You are a natural.

00:01:30.575 --> 00:01:33.328
Come with me. I have something to show you.

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:37.708
BEA: I can't believe you're giving this to me!

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:39.960
I haven't even written a best seller yet. Ha!

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.920
That's how confident I am in the changes

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.799
you're making to the new book. Have you seen Marvin's latest?

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:48.927
Oh. Wow. Is it good? Well, the critics have been

00:01:48.927 --> 00:01:52.514
mixed on it, but it just hit number one in 23 countries,

00:01:52.514 --> 00:01:55.559
including Germany, and they hate butterflies.

00:01:55.559 --> 00:01:59.061
Find them too whimsical. I want you to meet the whole company.

00:01:59.061 --> 00:02:01.648
Branding, merchandising, film.

00:02:01.648 --> 00:02:03.984
We don't just think of this as a book anymore.

00:02:03.984 --> 00:02:08.321
We think it's a phenomenon. We think you are a phenomenon.

00:02:08.321 --> 00:02:10.574
NARRATOR: A phenomenon, indeed.

00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:12.951
But also a phenomenon with a husband

00:02:12.951 --> 00:02:14.912
who thought she might be traveling

00:02:14.912 --> 00:02:16.872
down the wrong path.

00:02:16.872 --> 00:02:19.833
So he went for his own meeting with the publisher

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:22.377
to do what he was sure was right for his wife.

00:02:22.377 --> 00:02:23.921
THOMAS: Nigel!

00:02:23.921 --> 00:02:25.881
But without telling her, of course.

00:02:25.881 --> 00:02:28.341
Thomas? What a lovely surprise.

00:02:28.341 --> 00:02:30.552
Twice in one day.

00:02:30.552 --> 00:02:33.430
I know what you're up to, okay? And it is very clear to me

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.724
you are trying to impose your will on my wife.

00:02:35.724 --> 00:02:37.434
I wanted to tell you to your face--

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:40.562
I'm heading to the gym for a few rounds. You box?

00:02:40.562 --> 00:02:42.814
Uh, yeah. Yeah, I told you I boxed, didn't I?

00:02:42.814 --> 00:02:44.399
Come with me. The only thing is

00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:46.317
that I haven't got my gear, so...

00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:47.819
Not to worry. We'll fix you up.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:48.946
Really? Come.

00:02:48.946 --> 00:02:50.781
Okay. Yep.

00:02:50.781 --> 00:02:56.453
[GRUNTING]

00:02:56.453 --> 00:02:58.789
[BOXERS GRUNTING] [SHOES SQUEAKING]

00:02:58.789 --> 00:03:00.331
[GRUNTING RAPIDLY]

00:03:00.331 --> 00:03:02.250
[EXHALING RHYTHMICALLY]

00:03:06.713 --> 00:03:09.883
Hey, Nigel. Uh, thank you again for the gear.

00:03:09.883 --> 00:03:12.427
Oh, my niece was very happy to lend it to you.

00:03:12.427 --> 00:03:13.720
[GRUNTING RAPIDLY]

00:03:14.763 --> 00:03:17.265
[GRUNTING RAPIDLY, SPLUTTERS]

00:03:17.265 --> 00:03:19.768
Listen, I need to talk to you about Bea.

00:03:19.768 --> 00:03:21.603
Whoa! Oh! Ha-ha-ha!

00:03:21.603 --> 00:03:23.772
You have her doubting her own ideas.

00:03:23.772 --> 00:03:25.899
Well, I want her to take on some of mine.

00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:27.943
Yeah. I can't help but feel though

00:03:27.943 --> 00:03:30.194
that she's losing her way.

00:03:30.194 --> 00:03:31.362
No. No?

00:03:31.362 --> 00:03:32.572
Losing your way

00:03:32.572 --> 00:03:34.032
is when no one buys your book

00:03:34.032 --> 00:03:35.826
because it's too niche. Ow!

00:03:35.826 --> 00:03:38.787
You say "niche"? It's pronounced niche!

00:03:38.787 --> 00:03:41.498
Whoa! Ha-ha-ha! And losing your way

00:03:41.498 --> 00:03:43.583
is when you want to have children

00:03:43.583 --> 00:03:45.043
but your wife is too busy

00:03:45.043 --> 00:03:48.880
painting pictures of rabbits going to space! Oh!

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.175
Space? Really? She's doing that?

00:03:53.175 --> 00:03:54.344
[GROANS]

00:03:56.138 --> 00:03:58.348
Oh, Nigel. Nigel, I'm so sorry.

00:03:59.265 --> 00:04:00.517
No, I'm sorry.

00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:03.061
But if you're that selfish,

00:04:03.061 --> 00:04:06.064
I don't know if you're cut out to be a father.

00:04:06.064 --> 00:04:07.231
Huh?

00:04:07.231 --> 00:04:08.733
[ENGINE REVVING]

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.989
[ENGINE SHUTS OFF]

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:15.281
Why did you tell Nigel

00:04:15.281 --> 00:04:17.200
that I didn't want his ideas? Oh...

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.910
No, no, no, I was just saying,

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:20.912
I felt you were losing your voice and integrity.

00:04:20.912 --> 00:04:23.040
But, clearly, I had nothing to worry about.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.666
You have no right to speak for me.

00:04:24.666 --> 00:04:26.376
Nigel said he was going to reduce

00:04:26.376 --> 00:04:28.586
the run of the second book. It's too "niche"?

00:04:28.586 --> 00:04:29.921
It's pronounced niche.

00:04:29.921 --> 00:04:32.298
If you're a pretentious twit.

00:04:32.298 --> 00:04:34.009
What, like Nigel Basil-Jones?

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:35.677
How many names does one man need?

00:04:35.677 --> 00:04:36.970
Nigel's parents...

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.638
NARRATOR: This went on for a while,

00:04:38.638 --> 00:04:41.641
as most arguments between grown-ups do.

00:04:41.641 --> 00:04:44.310
And they start to say things that have nothing to do

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.521
with what they're actually arguing about.

00:04:46.521 --> 00:04:49.191
Brussels is the capital of Belgium.

00:04:49.191 --> 00:04:52.110
Capitals have moved before, Bea. Capitals will move again.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.071
Kyoto, Japan. 1869.

00:04:54.071 --> 00:04:56.364
Where are those moving trucks going? Tokyo!

00:04:56.364 --> 00:04:58.742
And often, the argument lasts a week

00:04:58.742 --> 00:05:00.994
before they get to the heart of the matter.

00:05:00.994 --> 00:05:04.372
But this is a storybook, so let's get right to it.

00:05:04.372 --> 00:05:05.957
I'm finally getting some success.

00:05:05.957 --> 00:05:07.584
I'm determined to hold on to it.

00:05:07.584 --> 00:05:09.127
If you could please support me

00:05:09.127 --> 00:05:12.172
instead of going behind my back, okay?

00:05:12.172 --> 00:05:14.424
You're right. I shouldn't have done that.

00:05:14.424 --> 00:05:16.634
But this isn't you, Bea.

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:19.930
You paint elegant stories of the rabbits and our home.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.933
I'm painting our family. It's... Just happens to be

00:05:22.933 --> 00:05:26.186
the best-selling version of our family.

00:05:26.186 --> 00:05:28.479
Nigel knows what he's doing.

00:05:31.775 --> 00:05:33.359
I love the new you.

00:05:33.359 --> 00:05:34.819
I can do anything to you

00:05:34.819 --> 00:05:37.781
and you won't eat me. Flick.

00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:41.409
Actually, today is my cheat day.

00:05:41.409 --> 00:05:42.744
[SCREAMS] [GROWLING]

00:05:42.744 --> 00:05:44.454
Help! Where is everybody?!

00:05:44.454 --> 00:05:46.790
NARRATOR: About to pull off the greatest

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:50.043
farmers-market heist in history.

00:05:50.043 --> 00:05:51.711
Also, the first.

00:05:51.711 --> 00:05:52.921
[BABBLES, GRUNTS]

00:05:52.921 --> 00:05:54.256
But Peter's always been

00:05:54.256 --> 00:05:57.884
a trailblazer, for better or worse.

00:05:57.884 --> 00:06:00.386
This little piggy went to market.

00:06:00.386 --> 00:06:03.431
For some lavender soap. I was told there was a lavender stand?

00:06:03.431 --> 00:06:05.016
Ugh. Hm?

00:06:05.016 --> 00:06:08.519
Peter, may I have a quick word?

00:06:10.939 --> 00:06:13.316
Are you sure about this? Yeah.

00:06:13.316 --> 00:06:16.194
I've never been more sure about anything in my life.

00:06:16.194 --> 00:06:18.780
Trust me. I would never put you in danger.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.158
ALL: Hm... Again.

00:06:21.158 --> 00:06:23.493
ALL: Hm? I promise.

00:06:23.493 --> 00:06:25.745
Mm-hm. Okay, I can't promise.

00:06:25.745 --> 00:06:27.831
We're gonna rob this place. You in or out?

00:06:27.831 --> 00:06:29.874
MITTENS: Ca-caw! Ca-caw!

00:06:29.874 --> 00:06:31.835
Ca-caw! Ca-caw!

00:06:31.835 --> 00:06:35.088
Ca-caw, ca-caw! BARNABAS: It's time.

00:06:35.088 --> 00:06:37.090
TOM: Ca-caw! MITTENS: Ca-caw!

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.176
BARNABAS: You know what to do. Yes.

00:06:39.176 --> 00:06:43.096
We steal the tailor's sausage and kiss Sara Nakamoto.

00:06:43.096 --> 00:06:44.306
What? No.

00:06:44.306 --> 00:06:45.640
We take out all the farmers

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.309
so we can get to the dried fruit

00:06:47.309 --> 00:06:49.393
and drag it back to the tailor's truck.

00:06:49.393 --> 00:06:51.604
Ah, okay. Wait a minute.

00:06:51.604 --> 00:06:53.773
I smell a rat. Huh?

00:06:53.773 --> 00:06:55.150
And I like it.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:56.526
Mmm. Heh-heh.

00:06:56.526 --> 00:06:58.820
[♪♪♪]

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.405
[GRUNTS, SHOUTS]

00:07:02.406 --> 00:07:03.616
[ANIMALS WHOOPING]

